00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.160
In the next few minutes,
I'm going to talk to you

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.780
about how you can ingest,
process, and route the events

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.060
in real-time into Fabric.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:31.230
.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.610
As Tessa mentioned in her
section, we get a lot

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.670
of different sources that
you can actually ingest your

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:39.870
data from.

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.510
We recognize that a lot of your
data actually starts outside

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:43.620
of Fabric.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:46.680
They are real-time in nature,
and while you could write a lot

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.690
of code to get the data in, we
provide a managed experience

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:53.610
through a whole
bunch of connectors.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.910
It's close to 40 of them
now that you can start

00:05:56.910 --> 00:06:01.500
with simple click and
drag and drop experiences

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.120
to get your connectors
into your system

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:09.090
and then use them
to ingest the data.

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.280
From all those different
sources, you're going

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:13.620
to route -- the messages
are going to get routed

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.490
to an Eventstream, which
actually exposes both the Kafka

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.880
and AMQP endpoints, so a
lot of flexibility there,

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.640
whether you have got open-source
clients or you're using AMQP.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.250
Once the data is ingested,
then you've got a couple

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:34.890
of different ways to process the
events while they're in motion.

00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.460
If you're familiar with
SQL, then we have a SQL way

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.690
of creating and transforming
and processing that data,

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:46.380
or you could even opt for a
no-code experience, which is,

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.680
again, just a simple drag and
drop of different operators

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.140
onto a surface to process.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.650
Now, as the events are going
in, they have a structure,

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.870
they transform,
they change shape.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:01.650
All of those can be registered
into a schema registry

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:02.850
and then managed from there.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.220
It provides you the
governance capabilities

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.090
as the data is moving
through your system,

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.430
but then once you're
done processing

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:15.270
and transforming your events,
where does it go next?

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:20.370
You have choices, a bunch of
choices, including Eventhouse,

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:24.390
which is our high-performance,
low-latency,

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:28.260
high-volume database
for time series data.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:32.220
Anshul will talk about
that a lot more in detail.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:36.330
Once the data is in Eventhouse,
you can do your analytics,

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.210
but it also provides --
Eventstream also provides a way

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.000
to integrate the output
of your stream processing

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.590
with other systems.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:45.210
For example, you can
actually send it

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:46.950
to another Kafka endpoint

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:50.970
if that's what your
application requires.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:54.240
Now, you can also
act on the data,

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:59.220
and this is where you can set up
rules and use activator to act

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.040
and integrate with
other systems as well.

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:05.640
Last, but not the least, I
wanted to talk about Spark.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:09.420
As the data is coming in
and you have your code,

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:14.970
stream processing code, all in
Spark, particularly in Python,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:18.720
then you can actually use Fabric
Spark and Fabric Notebooks

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.200
to pull the data from
Eventstream, process it,

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:25.800
and send it to further
downstream endpoints

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.650
that Spark can actually talk to.

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:33.720
That's the end-to-end, but this
is all in a high-level picture.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:39.510
Let's see how it all comes
together in a short demo.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:45.120
All of us have been to a stadium
for concerts, for games.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.130
Next month, we are going to have
a whole bunch of soccer games

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:53.580
around the country, around
the continent, actually.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.130
While we as fans go to
the stadium and expect

00:08:56.130 --> 00:09:00.880
to have a good time, there's a
lot going on in that stadium.

00:09:02.610 --> 00:09:06.210
As fans are coming
in, as they are going

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:10.080
through the turnstiles,
they're purchasing concessions

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:13.050
from the concession stands
and enjoying the game.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:15.980
There are lots of different
points where data is going

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:19.820
to get generated, hundreds
of different points

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:23.780
from where data is generated,
but also, thousands of events

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:27.460
that are actually happening
every single second.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.210
The challenge of the game
day operations people have

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:33.750
at the stadium is how to
collect all of that data,

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:38.940
make sense of the data,
and be able to take action

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:42.210
within minutes, if not
sometimes in seconds.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.420
That's what I will walk you
through how to accomplish

00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:50.170
that with RTI and
with Event Streams.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:54.000
Let me show you what the
developer journey now

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:55.080
looks like.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:57.900
I'm going to start in
the real-time hub,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:02.760
which shows me all the different
streaming assets that I have.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:05.400
I'm going to go to
the Add Data section,

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.070
and you can see the
dozens of connectors

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:11.160
that we already have
made available.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:15.510
In this case, I'm going to
pick the MQTT connector,

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:19.290
as for my turnstile,
MQTT is a great protocol

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.510
for getting events
from my turnstile.

00:10:21.510 --> 00:10:25.620
I'm just going to set up a
secure connection to the broker,

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.920
MQTT broker, and then I'm
going to set up the topic name

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:32.720
and then choose
the MQTT version.

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:38.310
In this case, V3, which is very
widely used in the IoT circles.

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.640
I'm going to quickly review what
the connection details are,

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.450
and that's it.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:45.780
In just a few simple clicks,
I now have an Eventstream

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:50.580
that is actually ingesting
data from all my turnstiles.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.420
Next up, I'm going to go
and process that event.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.730
I'm going to go and
do an edit mode,

00:10:56.730 --> 00:11:00.930
launch the SQL code operator
that I've mentioned earlier,

00:11:00.930 --> 00:11:03.360
and then start writing the code.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:05.190
You can see the edit query.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:08.910
It starts you off with a very
simple "Select Star" query

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.610
that I can actually
now start to edit.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.620
I'm just going to -- I've
written some code already,

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:16.320
maybe with some agents.

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.890
I'm going to move that here,
and you can start to see

00:11:19.890 --> 00:11:23.880
that I can do, again, using
very familiar SQL syntax.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.430
Start to write the query,
do some simple filtering,

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:29.370
and route to different
destinations there.

00:11:29.370 --> 00:11:31.860
Now, one important thing
here, without having to move

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:34.020
to production, I can
actually test the query

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:37.350
from the editor itself,
which is a great help

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:40.080
and makes things a
lot more productive

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:42.210
as you're developing
your complex jobs

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.730
that you might have
in your environment.

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.400
I'm going to go ahead and save.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:51.760
I like what I see here, so
I'm going to save the query

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:56.580
and publish the Eventstream,

00:11:56.580 --> 00:11:59.160
and so now the Eventstream
is actually live.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:00.570
It is getting the events.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.820
It's getting processed
and getting delivered

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.670
into the Eventhouse
destinations that I've set up.

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.910
Let me go ahead and open the
Eventhouse, and I can verify

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.670
that all the turnstile
passage events are showing

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:15.030
up as I expected, so great.

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.950
Now, I'm going to go back,

00:12:16.950 --> 00:12:19.920
and then I'm going to
see the next step.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:22.710
Now, in a complex environment,

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:24.810
in your actual game
day operations,

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.310
lots of things happen.

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:29.970
Events arrive late, they arrive
out of order, and you need

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:32.190
to handle those situations.

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:35.670
With Eventstreams, we have
actually made it very simple.

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.370
Just set up a few
simple policies,

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:42.480
and it will guide how
Eventstream deals

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:48.090
with the events that might
show up late or out of order.

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:51.090
Now I'm going to go into
my Eventstream again

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.000
and do something more
interesting, which is, actually,

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.180
try to detect duplicate
tickets being used.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:12:58.680
That's fraud.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:02.550
With very simple window
aggregations, this time,

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.240
I'm actually using a hopping
window that within 120 seconds,

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:10.980
if the same ticket gets used
in different gates or even

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:13.080
at the same gate,
then that's something

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:17.760
that Eventstream will be able
to detect and warn you about.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:18.300
That's it.

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:23.340
I've set up my second
SQL code operator,

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:27.870
which can detect very
simple fraud and alert me

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.880
in a very short amount of time.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:31.950
Now I'm going to just
go and set the alert.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:35.580
I'm going to bring up the
activator UI and each time

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:40.380
that a fraud or duplicate ticket
gets used, I'm going to go ahead

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:42.750
